::ENG1_U01_L04_Q01::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Passage: The school opened the gym early on rainy mornings. Students could wait indoors instead of standing outside before the first bell. Question: Which response is strongest?{
~A. The gym was at school, and rain happened.#Incorrect. This is too vague and does not explain the benefit.
~B. Students stood outside because the gym was closed forever.#Incorrect. The passage says the gym opened early.
~C. The first bell rang because it was raining.#Incorrect. This does not explain the gym's purpose.
=D. Opening the gym early helped students stay dry because they could wait indoors before the first bell.#Correct. This answers the question and uses evidence from the passage.
}

::ENG1_U01_L04_Q02::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Passage: The library placed return carts at both entrances. Students could return books quickly no matter which door they used. Question: Which evidence-based response is best?{
~A. The library had doors, so students had books.#Incorrect. This is not a clear evidence-based response.
=B. The return carts made returning books easier because students could use either entrance.#Correct. This paraphrases the benefit and uses relevant evidence.
~C. Students returned carts because the books were quick.#Incorrect. This changes the meaning.
~D. The entrances were in the library, and books existed.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
}

::ENG1_U01_L04_Q03::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Passage: The teacher posted the day's agenda before class. Students knew which materials to take out and what task to begin first. Question: Which response best explains why the agenda helped?{
=A. The agenda helped students prepare because it told them what materials and first task they needed.#Correct. This response answers the question with evidence.
~B. The agenda was posted before class, so the teacher had a board.#Incorrect. This does not explain the benefit.
~C. Students had materials and tasks in the room.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~D. The teacher posted something because class started.#Incorrect. This is not a clear explanation.
}

::ENG1_U01_L04_Q04::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Passage: The soccer team used colored cones to mark each practice station. Players moved to the correct area without waiting for the coach to repeat directions. Question: Which response is strongest?{
~A. The cones were colored and soccer existed.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
~B. Players waited because the coach repeated directions.#Incorrect. The passage says players moved without waiting.
=C. Colored cones helped players find practice stations without waiting for repeated directions.#Correct. This paraphrases the benefit and includes evidence.
~D. The coach removed directions because cones were colorful.#Incorrect. This changes the meaning.
}

::ENG1_U01_L04_Q05::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Passage: The cafeteria placed fruit baskets near the checkout line. Students were more likely to add fruit to lunch because it was easy to see and reach. Question: Which response best explains the result?{
~A. Students checked out because baskets had fruit.#Incorrect. This is unclear and does not explain the result.
=B. Placing fruit near checkout encouraged students to choose it because it was visible and easy to reach.#Correct. This clearly explains the result using evidence.
~C. The cafeteria had lunch, so fruit was nearby.#Incorrect. This is too general.
~D. Fruit baskets made the checkout line disappear.#Incorrect. The passage does not say the line disappeared.
}

::ENG1_U01_L04_Q06::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Passage: The teacher allowed students to preview discussion questions before reading. Students said the questions helped them notice important details in the article. Question: Which response is best?{
~A. Students read because questions were there.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
~B. The teacher removed the article after giving questions.#Incorrect. The passage does not say the article was removed.
~C. Students wrote questions because details were not important.#Incorrect. This contradicts the passage.
=D. Previewing the questions helped students focus on important article details while reading.#Correct. This response paraphrases and explains the evidence.
}

::ENG1_U01_L04_Q07::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Passage: The school store offered supply kits with pencils, folders, and notebooks together. Families could buy the required items in one purchase. Question: Which response is strongest?{
=A. The supply kits helped families buy required school materials in one purchase.#Correct. This accurately paraphrases the benefit.
~B. Families bought stores because notebooks were together.#Incorrect. This is unclear and changes the meaning.
~C. The school store removed pencils from the kits.#Incorrect. Pencils were included in the kits.
~D. Required items were not available to families.#Incorrect. The passage says families could buy them.
}

::ENG1_U01_L04_Q08::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Passage: The science teacher gave students goggles before the experiment began. The goggles protected students' eyes while they mixed liquids. Question: Which sentence best explains the evidence?{
~A. The goggles were before the experiment, and liquids were there.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
~B. The experiment protected goggles from students.#Incorrect. This reverses the meaning.
=C. The goggles helped keep students safe because they protected students' eyes during the experiment.#Correct. This explains the benefit with evidence.
~D. Students mixed goggles with liquids.#Incorrect. This misreads the passage.
}

::ENG1_U01_L04_Q09::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Passage: The town added a covered bench at the bus stop. Riders could sit and stay out of the rain while they waited. Question: Which response is best?{
=A. The covered bench improved the bus stop because riders could sit and stay dry while waiting.#Correct. This response is clear and evidence-based.
~B. The bus stop had rain because riders sat down.#Incorrect. This confuses cause and effect.
~C. The bench waited for riders at the town.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
~D. Riders stopped using the bus because the bench was covered.#Incorrect. The passage does not say riders stopped using the bus.
}

::ENG1_U01_L04_Q10::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Passage: The music teacher recorded the rhythm pattern and posted it online. Students practiced at home by clapping along with the recording. Question: Which response best explains how the recording helped?{
~A. The recording was online because rhythm existed.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
~B. Students clapped because the teacher was music.#Incorrect. This is not clear or evidence-based.
=C. The recording helped students practice the rhythm at home by clapping along with it.#Correct. This answers the question with evidence.
~D. The music teacher stopped teaching rhythm.#Incorrect. The recording supported practice.
}

::ENG1_U01_L04_Q11::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Passage: The principal sent weekly email reminders about club deadlines. Students had more time to gather signatures and complete forms before applications were due. Question: Which response is strongest?{
~A. The principal emailed because clubs were weekly.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
=B. Weekly reminders helped students prepare applications because they had more time to gather signatures and complete forms.#Correct. This uses evidence to explain the benefit.
~C. Students missed every deadline because emails were sent.#Incorrect. This contradicts the passage.
~D. Applications were due because signatures had clubs.#Incorrect. This is not a meaningful response.
}

::ENG1_U01_L04_Q12::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Passage: The city painted crosswalks in brighter colors near the elementary school. Drivers could see the crossing areas more clearly during morning traffic. Question: Which response best explains the effect?{
~A. The crosswalks were colors near school.#Incorrect. This is incomplete and vague.
~B. Morning traffic painted the elementary school.#Incorrect. This changes the meaning.
~C. Drivers were removed from crossing areas.#Incorrect. The passage does not say drivers were removed.
=D. Brighter crosswalks helped drivers see crossing areas more clearly during morning traffic.#Correct. This paraphrases the effect and uses evidence.
}

::ENG1_U01_L04_Q13::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Passage: The classroom added a question box for students who were nervous about speaking aloud. The teacher answered the questions at the end of class without naming students. Question: Which response is best?{
~A. Students stopped asking questions because the box had no names.#Incorrect. The passage says the box helped students ask.
~B. The teacher named every student at the end of class.#Incorrect. The passage says the teacher did not name students.
~C. The box was in the classroom, so questions were nervous.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
=D. The question box helped nervous students ask questions privately because the teacher answered without naming them.#Correct. This response explains the benefit with evidence.
}

::ENG1_U01_L04_Q14::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Passage: The art club displayed project examples beside the supply table. Students used the examples to understand how the finished project could look. Question: Which response is strongest?{
~A. The supply table was beside examples.#Incorrect. This gives location but not the purpose.
=B. The examples helped students understand the final project because they showed how it could look.#Correct. This is clear and evidence-based.
~C. Students finished the project before seeing examples.#Incorrect. The passage does not say this.
~D. The art club removed supplies from the table.#Incorrect. This contradicts the passage.
}

::ENG1_U01_L04_Q15::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Passage: The shelter posted photos of lost pets online each afternoon. Owners could check the website before driving to the shelter. Question: Which response best explains how the website helped?{
~A. The shelter had afternoons and photos.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
~B. Owners drove because the website was lost.#Incorrect. This changes the meaning.
=C. Posting photos online helped owners check for lost pets before going to the shelter.#Correct. This response accurately explains the benefit.
~D. The website stopped owners from finding pets.#Incorrect. The passage says the website helped owners check.
}

::ENG1_U01_L04_Q16::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Passage: The teacher numbered each step of the lab directions. Students followed the steps in order and made fewer mistakes during the experiment. Question: Which response is best?{
=A. Numbered directions helped students follow the lab in order and make fewer mistakes.#Correct. This response includes the paraphrase and evidence.
~B. The lab directions had numbers because experiments existed.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~C. Students made more mistakes because directions were numbered.#Incorrect. The passage says fewer mistakes happened.
~D. The teacher removed steps from the lab.#Incorrect. The teacher numbered the steps.
}

::ENG1_U01_L04_Q17::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Passage: The community garden added labels to each row of plants. Visitors could tell which vegetables were growing without asking a volunteer. Question: Which response is strongest?{
~A. Volunteers asked vegetables to grow in rows.#Incorrect. This is not supported by the passage.
=B. Plant labels helped visitors identify vegetables without needing to ask a volunteer.#Correct. This response is clear, accurate, and evidence-based.
~C. The labels made visitors stop going to the garden.#Incorrect. The passage does not say that.
~D. Rows of plants had labels, and labels were there.#Incorrect. This is repetitive and does not explain the benefit.
}

::ENG1_U01_L04_Q18::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Passage: The coach shared a stretching chart before practice. Athletes used the chart to warm up safely without waiting for individual instructions. Question: Which response best explains the chart's purpose?{
~A. The chart was before practice, so athletes waited.#Incorrect. The chart helped them avoid waiting for individual instructions.
~B. Athletes stretched because charts are paper.#Incorrect. This is not a clear explanation.
=C. The chart helped athletes warm up safely and independently before practice.#Correct. This response paraphrases the purpose and uses evidence.
~D. The coach stopped practice because stretching existed.#Incorrect. The passage does not say practice stopped.
}

::ENG1_U01_L04_Q19::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Passage: The school website added a translation button for family announcements. Families could read important updates in the language they understood best. Question: Which response is best?{
~A. The website had a button because families had announcements.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~B. Families read languages because the website was important.#Incorrect. This does not explain the button's purpose.
~C. The website removed family announcements.#Incorrect. The passage says announcements were available with translation.
=D. The translation button helped families understand announcements by letting them read updates in their best language.#Correct. This response is clear and evidence-based.
}

::ENG1_U01_L04_Q20::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Passage: The teacher gave a sample paragraph before independent writing. Students used the sample to see how a topic sentence, evidence, and explanation fit together. Question: Which response is strongest?{
=A. The sample paragraph helped students understand how parts of a response fit together.#Correct. This accurately explains the benefit.
~B. Students copied the paragraph because independent writing stopped.#Incorrect. The sample supported writing; it did not stop it.
~C. The teacher removed evidence from the sample.#Incorrect. Evidence was one part of the sample.
~D. Topic sentences fit because writing was independent.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
}

::ENG1_U01_L04_Q21::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Passage: The park added trash and recycling bins near picnic tables. Visitors had a place to sort waste before leaving the area. Question: Which response best explains the change?{
~A. Visitors left because picnic tables had waste.#Incorrect. This changes the meaning.
~B. The park removed picnic tables because bins were nearby.#Incorrect. The passage does not say tables were removed.
~C. Trash and recycling became the same thing.#Incorrect. The bins helped sort waste.
=D. The bins helped visitors sort waste near the picnic area before leaving.#Correct. This response paraphrases the change and uses evidence.
}

::ENG1_U01_L04_Q22::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Passage: The teacher highlighted key dates on the project calendar. Students could see when research notes, drafts, and final copies were due. Question: Which response is best?{
~A. The calendar was highlighted because projects existed.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
~B. Students finished final copies before research notes.#Incorrect. The passage does not say this.
=C. Highlighted dates helped students track when each part of the project was due.#Correct. This explains the benefit using evidence.
~D. The teacher removed due dates from the calendar.#Incorrect. The teacher highlighted due dates.
}

::ENG1_U01_L04_Q23::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Passage: The museum placed audio guides beside the entrance. Visitors could listen to explanations while viewing each exhibit. Question: Which response best explains the purpose?{
~A. The entrance listened to audio guides.#Incorrect. This is not logical.
=B. Audio guides helped visitors understand exhibits by providing explanations as they viewed them.#Correct. This is clear and evidence-based.
~C. Visitors viewed exhibits because the entrance was beside them.#Incorrect. This does not explain the audio guides.
~D. The museum removed explanations from the exhibits.#Incorrect. The audio guides provided explanations.
}

::ENG1_U01_L04_Q24::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Passage: The cafeteria posted ingredient lists for each meal. Students with food allergies could check the lists before choosing lunch. Question: Which response is strongest?{
~A. Students with allergies could not eat because lists existed.#Incorrect. The lists helped students choose.
~B. The cafeteria removed all meals from lunch.#Incorrect. The passage does not say meals were removed.
=C. Ingredient lists helped students with allergies choose lunch safely.#Correct. This accurately explains the benefit.
~D. The lists chose lunch for each student.#Incorrect. Students checked the lists before choosing.
}

::ENG1_U01_L04_Q25::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Passage: The teacher added sentence starters to the discussion board. Students used the starters to explain agreement, disagreement, and evidence more clearly. Question: Which response is best?{
=A. Sentence starters helped students write clearer discussion responses about agreement, disagreement, and evidence.#Correct. This response answers the question and uses evidence.
~B. Students stopped discussing because sentence starters were posted.#Incorrect. The starters supported discussion.
~C. The discussion board removed evidence from student responses.#Incorrect. The passage says starters helped students explain evidence.
~D. Sentence starters were unclear because students used them.#Incorrect. The passage says they helped students explain more clearly.
}
